WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:09.550
This is Chapter 12: the managed
service account standard.

4
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.272
A standard nobody checks is a suggestion,

5
00:00:12.272 --> 00:00:17.636
so this chapter checks it every time
something changes: a request, an aggregation,

6
00:00:17.636 --> 00:00:23.000
a certification, a Defender for Identity
scan, a disabled account's ninetieth day.

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.967
We'll see what the products give the
code, the import formats, the five checks,

8
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:31.492
what the code hands to Chapter
6 and what it never does,

9
00:00:31.492 --> 00:00:33.975
the evidence, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:39.490
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.002
Why does the standard need code?

12
00:00:45.002 --> 00:00:46.902
Because every step can drift.

13
00:00:46.902 --> 00:00:52.117
Each group or delegated managed service
account is asked for in the agency's system

14
00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:56.576
access management tool, approved in
SailPoint, recorded in ServiceNow,

15
00:00:56.576 --> 00:01:01.640
created by the pipeline through Chapter
6's approved plan, watched with Defender

16
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.477
for Identity, certified by its owners,
and retired after ninety days disabled.

17
00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:10.253
Chapter 6 is the only part that
writes to Active Directory.

18
00:01:10.253 --> 00:01:12.515
This chapter writes nothing anywhere.

19
00:01:12.515 --> 00:01:17.260
It checks, compares and lists, and
leaves the evidence for each step.

20
00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.938
The standard's rule is
short: machines run,

21
00:01:23.938 --> 00:01:29.535
check and record every step they can, and
people decide, approve and accept risk.

22
00:01:29.535 --> 00:01:33.701
A request reaches the approvers
only after the code has checked it,

23
00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:37.185
so they decide on purpose and
risk, not on a naming prefix.

24
00:01:37.185 --> 00:01:40.254
The code writes the rows
Chapter 6's plan reads,

25
00:01:40.254 --> 00:01:43.323
so the plan creates
exactly what was approved.

26
00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:45.974
Every account is compared on every run,

27
00:01:45.974 --> 00:01:49.123
so an unrequested one is
a finding the same day.

28
00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:51.685
Certifications follow risk tiers.

29
00:01:51.685 --> 00:01:56.900
And the code never deletes: it says which
accounts a person may delete, and why.

30
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.138
What does the code depend on?

31
00:02:02.138 --> 00:02:06.930
First, the standard itself: names,
the organizational unit, groups,

32
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.876
the password interval, owners and cadences,
all decided in the decision register.

33
00:02:12.876 --> 00:02:15.107
SailPoint approves every request,

34
00:02:15.107 --> 00:02:18.725
and ServiceNow holds the change
record and the findings.

35
00:02:18.725 --> 00:02:23.598
SailPoint's documentation says its Active
Directory connector can aggregate managed

36
00:02:23.598 --> 00:02:25.935
service accounts, and that reading who may

37
00:02:25.935 --> 00:02:28.338
retrieve the password needs its IQService.

38
00:02:28.338 --> 00:02:33.432
On the Microsoft side, Learn says Defender
for Identity lists service accounts by

39
00:02:33.432 --> 00:02:36.279
type, its stale account
check uses ninety days,

40
00:02:36.279 --> 00:02:39.560
and the password interval
is fixed at creation.

41
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.971
The product pages read on
November twentieth didn't specify

42
00:02:46.971 --> 00:02:50.485
SailPoint's or Defender for
Identity's export field names.

43
00:02:50.485 --> 00:02:55.285
So the kit reads its own import formats
and says so in each file's header.

44
00:02:55.285 --> 00:02:58.728
There are six: the approved
requests, one file each;

45
00:02:58.728 --> 00:03:02.926
SailPoint's aggregated accounts;
the Active Directory read,

46
00:03:02.926 --> 00:03:07.881
a fixture in Lab 1 and live in Lab 2;
the program's register; a certification

47
00:03:07.881 --> 00:03:12.835
campaign's decisions; and Defender
for Identity's service account list.

48
00:03:12.835 --> 00:03:17.520
In Lab 2, you map each product
column to the kit's column by hand.

49
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.595
So what does the code add?

50
00:03:22.595 --> 00:03:26.495
One script and one module,
running one read-only task.

51
00:03:26.495 --> 00:03:31.920
It makes exactly one Active Directory
call, Get A D Service Account, and that

52
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.257
call is declared in a config file, as are
the controls and K S I's for each step.

53
00:03:37.257 --> 00:03:42.151
Every run writes a folder: the findings,
the generated inputs for Chapter 6,

54
00:03:42.151 --> 00:03:46.563
the certification, Defender for
Identity and retirement results,

55
00:03:46.563 --> 00:03:51.858
the ServiceNow findings, the evidence rows,
and a manifest with each input's hash.

56
00:03:51.858 --> 00:03:55.740
The same inputs and date
give byte-identical files.

57
00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.878
The first check is the
request, findings one to nine.

58
00:04:02.878 --> 00:04:07.553
A request has to be approved in SailPoint
and carry a ServiceNow change record.

59
00:04:07.553 --> 00:04:09.728
The name has to follow the name standard.

60
00:04:09.728 --> 00:04:14.665
It needs exactly two owners, and a
federal-employee sponsor who isn't one of them.

61
00:04:14.665 --> 00:04:18.502
A group managed account needs its
hosts group, named for the app.

62
00:04:18.502 --> 00:04:21.614
The service principal names
have to match the request.

63
00:04:21.614 --> 00:04:24.465
A delegated managed
account has to be run by

64
00:04:24.465 --> 00:04:27.315
a person and supersede
an existing account.

65
00:04:27.315 --> 00:04:31.440
And the workload, type and risk
tier must be known values.

66
00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:39.448
The second check, findings ten to
nineteen, reconciles what SailPoint

67
00:04:39.448 --> 00:04:43.852
aggregated with what Active Directory
holds and what the register says.

68
00:04:43.852 --> 00:04:47.603
Every account must be aggregated
by SailPoint with one owner.

69
00:04:47.603 --> 00:04:51.440
Its password retrievers must be
a single domain local group.

70
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.066
The interval must be thirty days and the
encryption A E S two fifty-six only; Learn

71
00:04:57.066 --> 00:05:02.778
says the interval can be set only at creation
and A E S should always be configured.

72
00:05:02.778 --> 00:05:06.779
An account with no service principal
name must be outbound-only,

73
00:05:06.779 --> 00:05:08.365
or have a recorded reason.

74
00:05:08.365 --> 00:05:09.877
No privileged groups.

75
00:05:09.877 --> 00:05:11.752
The right organizational unit.

76
00:05:11.752 --> 00:05:14.502
And every account must be
explained by a request,

77
00:05:14.502 --> 00:05:17.610
which is the drift rule
from the governance book.

78
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.510
Three more checks.

79
00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:27.451
Certification is due by risk tier:
every three months for the highest tier,

80
00:05:27.451 --> 00:05:30.772
every six for the middle,
and yearly for the lowest.

81
00:05:30.772 --> 00:05:34.291
Each revoke becomes a ServiceNow
stale access finding,

82
00:05:34.291 --> 00:05:37.810
and a decision by someone who
isn't an owner doesn't count.

83
00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:42.462
The Defender for Identity check reads
its service account list and flags a user

84
00:05:42.462 --> 00:05:47.255
account still serving as a service account,
a standalone managed service account,

85
00:05:47.255 --> 00:05:51.273
and any account Defender for Identity
sees that the register doesn't.

86
00:05:51.273 --> 00:05:54.773
And retirement: an account
becomes a delete candidate

87
00:05:54.773 --> 00:05:58.550
only after ninety days disabled
with no sign-in since.

88
00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.230
For each request that passes, the code
writes the row Chapter 6's plan reads,

89
00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:12.888
with all twelve columns, including the
SailPoint request and the ServiceNow change.

90
00:06:12.888 --> 00:06:14.628
For a group managed account,

91
00:06:14.628 --> 00:06:18.412
it also names the one group
allowed to retrieve the password.

92
00:06:18.412 --> 00:06:23.922
Those files go into Chapter 6's reviewed
inputs through a reviewed pull request,

93
00:06:23.922 --> 00:06:29.013
and Chapter 6's plan refuses any write
without a request or a change record.

94
00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:32.062
And just as important is what
this chapter never does.

95
00:06:32.062 --> 00:06:35.755
It writes nothing to any of the
four products, it deletes nothing,

96
00:06:35.755 --> 00:06:39.940
and it builds the ServiceNow
findings payload without posting it.

97
00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.678
Every step leaves evidence.

98
00:06:45.678 --> 00:06:49.303
The request check supports
A C two and I A four.

99
00:06:49.303 --> 00:06:54.127
The generated inputs support C M
three, configuration change control.

100
00:06:54.127 --> 00:06:57.865
The reconciliation supports
inventory and least privilege.

101
00:06:57.865 --> 00:07:01.990
Certifications support account
management and the review of privileges.

102
00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.977
The Defender for Identity comparison
supports continuous monitoring.

103
00:07:05.977 --> 00:07:08.865
The retirement list supports
disabling accounts.

104
00:07:08.865 --> 00:07:11.677
And the passing tests
support testing changes.

105
00:07:11.677 --> 00:07:16.002
Each row also names the fed-ramp
twenty X K S I's aligned with it,

106
00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:19.116
re-read from FedRAMP
on November twentieth.

107
00:07:19.116 --> 00:07:24.265
Two cautions: a row provides evidence
for a control, and the assessor decides.

108
00:07:24.265 --> 00:07:27.334
And K S I's are indicators
for cloud providers;

109
00:07:27.334 --> 00:07:30.760
the agency lines its
evidence up with theirs.

110
00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.560
Who's involved?

111
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:40.810
The identity engineers who run Chapter
6's write path and own the standard.

112
00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:45.748
The SailPoint and ServiceNow administrators,
who export and import the files.

113
00:07:45.748 --> 00:07:48.922
And the security officer,
who reads the evidence.

114
00:07:48.922 --> 00:07:52.030
Approvers in SailPoint
decide on purpose and risk,

115
00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:54.398
after the code has checked the request.

116
00:07:54.398 --> 00:07:57.360
The two owners certify
on their tier's cadence,

117
00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.336
and the code schedules it and
turns each revoke into a finding.

118
00:08:01.336 --> 00:08:04.481
And a person deletes, through
the decommission ticket,

119
00:08:04.481 --> 00:08:06.660
from the list the code produced.

120
00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:14.147
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7
with Pester, and no domain.

121
00:08:14.147 --> 00:08:19.704
Step 1 runs the standard's checks on the
fixtures: five requests, of which two pass;

122
00:08:19.704 --> 00:08:25.259
nine Active Directory accounts; eight
register rows; a certification campaign;

123
00:08:25.259 --> 00:08:29.973
and a Defender for Identity list,
each built to trip one or more rules.

124
00:08:29.973 --> 00:08:34.911
The summary says twenty-five findings,
four warnings, three for information.

125
00:08:34.911 --> 00:08:38.160
Two Chapter 6 rows and
one retriever entry.

126
00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.010
One certification overdue.

127
00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:43.048
One account a person may
delete, and nothing deleted.

128
00:08:43.048 --> 00:08:46.610
Then one line per rule,
and the manifest's hash.

129
00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.422
Step 2 reads the findings.

130
00:08:52.422 --> 00:08:54.847
Look for the account called gmsa ops.

131
00:08:54.847 --> 00:08:58.277
It's in Domain Admins,
SailPoint never aggregated it,

132
00:08:58.277 --> 00:09:03.222
and no request explains it: three findings
for one account nobody asked for.

133
00:09:03.222 --> 00:09:06.360
Step 3 reads what the
code hands to Chapter 6.

134
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.646
Two rows, one per approved request,

135
00:09:08.646 --> 00:09:12.972
each carrying its SailPoint
request and its ServiceNow change.

136
00:09:12.972 --> 00:09:16.479
The approved group managed
account gets its retriever group,

137
00:09:16.479 --> 00:09:17.923
named for the payroll app.

138
00:09:17.923 --> 00:09:21.201
The delegated managed account
gets no retriever entry,

139
00:09:21.201 --> 00:09:24.470
because it learns its
hosts during its migration.

140
00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:30.645
Step 4 reads the certifications.

141
00:09:30.645 --> 00:09:33.070
The account gmsa print is overdue:

142
00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:37.748
it's in the lowest risk tier and
was last certified in October 2025,

143
00:09:37.748 --> 00:09:42.945
and its owner revoked it, so it becomes
a ServiceNow stale access finding.

144
00:09:42.945 --> 00:09:45.195
Step 5 is the retirement list.

145
00:09:45.195 --> 00:09:47.298
Only gmsa legacy may be deleted,

146
00:09:47.298 --> 00:09:50.857
and only by a person through
the decommission ticket.

147
00:09:50.857 --> 00:09:53.589
Another account signed in
after it was disabled,

148
00:09:53.589 --> 00:09:56.321
and a third is still
enabled in Active Directory.

149
00:09:56.321 --> 00:10:00.909
Step 6 reads the evidence:
one row per step and control,

150
00:10:00.909 --> 00:10:03.620
with its K S I's and the file's hash.

151
00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.370
Step 7 runs the tests: twenty-seven pass.

152
00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.037
Lab 2 uses a test
domain, never production,

153
00:10:14.037 --> 00:10:18.841
with Chapter 6's second lab already
done, a SailPoint sandbox scoped

154
00:10:18.841 --> 00:10:23.645
to the service account unit, and
Defender for Identity on the domain.

155
00:10:23.645 --> 00:10:25.207
The authors haven't run it.

156
00:10:25.207 --> 00:10:27.169
Write the test domain's settings.

157
00:10:27.169 --> 00:10:30.166
Then put the real exports
into the kit's formats,

158
00:10:30.166 --> 00:10:32.470
mapping each product column by hand.

159
00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:37.114
Run with a live read of the domain; a
group managed account you made by hand in

160
00:10:37.114 --> 00:10:40.485
Chapter 6 with no request
shows up as finding eighteen,

161
00:10:40.485 --> 00:10:42.433
which is the drift rule working.

162
00:10:42.433 --> 00:10:47.970
Finally, confirm the manifest shows one
read of Active Directory, and nothing else.

163
00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.170
Each danger has a guard.

164
00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:56.720
An approver might approve a
request that breaks the standard,

165
00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:01.383
so the check runs before approval, and
Chapter 6 refuses a write without a request.

166
00:11:01.383 --> 00:11:06.558
A group managed account created by hand
is caught by the drift rule on every run.

167
00:11:06.558 --> 00:11:11.772
If SailPoint correlates no owner, or
the wrong one, certifications go astray,

168
00:11:11.772 --> 00:11:15.332
so that's a finding, and one
attribute names one owner.

169
00:11:15.332 --> 00:11:18.195
A certification by a
non-owner doesn't count.

170
00:11:18.195 --> 00:11:23.145
A disabled account is deleted only
after ninety quiet days, by a person.

171
00:11:23.145 --> 00:11:25.947
And because the import
formats could drift from

172
00:11:25.947 --> 00:11:28.980
the products' exports,
every header is checked.

173
00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:34.080
Here's what done looks like.

174
00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:37.130
Every request was checked
before the approvers saw it.

175
00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:41.211
Every row in Chapter 6's inputs
came from an approved request,

176
00:11:41.211 --> 00:11:44.068
with its request number and change record.

177
00:11:44.068 --> 00:11:48.556
SailPoint aggregates every managed
account, each with one owner, and no

178
00:11:48.556 --> 00:11:53.805
reconciliation finding is open without a
ServiceNow finding and someone to fix it.

179
00:11:53.805 --> 00:11:58.711
Every certification is current, every
revoke is a finding, and Defender for

180
00:11:58.711 --> 00:12:03.855
Identity shows no user account serving as
a service account that isn't on a plan.

181
00:12:03.855 --> 00:12:07.280
Nothing was deleted except
by a person, from the list.

182
00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:11.630
And each run's evidence and manifest
are filed with the change record.
